# ============================================================================
#  Fitness — BodyLab + TrainingLab
#
#  Goal of this file: a clone should contain everything needed to *build* the
#  apps, and nothing that is only needed to develop *on this machine*.
#
#  Two rules drove the exclusions:
#    1. generated or vendored-by-a-tool directories (node_modules, dist, the
#       Rust target dir) are reproducible from the lockfiles — never commit them;
#    2. agent scratch, local diagnostics and machine-specific caches are not part
#       of the product and only add noise to a public repository.
# ============================================================================

# ── Dependencies ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/

# npm's lockfile is intentionally not used: this is a pnpm workspace, and two
# lockfiles in a repo is a reliable way to install two different trees.
package-lock.json

# ── Build output ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
dist/
dist-ssr/
build/
out/
*.tsbuildinfo
.vite/

# Rust / Tauri: the release binary is published as a GitHub Release asset, not
# as a source file. `src-tauri/target` is routinely several GB.
target/
**/src-tauri/target/
**/src-tauri/gen/
src-tauri/gen/

# ── Heavy working assets (NOT needed to build or run the shipped apps) ──────
#
# These are the interesting exclusions, and they are deliberate:
#
#   resources/anatomy/  ~410 MB of anatomy *working files* — the raw z-anatomy
#                       download, its .blend source and the GLB exports the
#                       pipeline produces. The app talks to the *compiled*
#                       assets already committed under apps/web/public/anatomy,
#                       so a clone builds and runs without any of this. The
#                       upstream model is CC-BY-SA and downloadable if you want
#                       to re-run the export pipeline (see the anatomy README).
#   corpus/             an internal research playground. knowledge.md states it
#                       is "not part of BodyLab's runtime"; its ~15 MB of
#                       MakeHuman data and compiled assets only serve that
#                       experiment, and its package is not needed for either app.
#   *.gif-work/         scratch directory for the exercise-GIF pipeline.
#
# Anything a release needs is either in the lockfiles or committed as a runtime
# asset (public/anatomy/*.glb, public/wasm/**, public/*.ohpk).
fitness-ecosystem/resources/anatomy/
fitness-ecosystem/corpus/
**/.gif-work/

# Rasterised brand artefacts: mastered from resources/brand/*.jpg and rebuilt by
# `node scripts/render-brand-icons.mjs`, so they are outputs, not sources.
**/*-icon-1024.png

# ── Third-party reference material (study only — never redistributed) ───────
#
# `docs/reference/` holds screenshots of *other* training apps (and any data
# exported from them) that we keep on this machine purely to study interface
# patterns. They are somebody else's copyrighted artwork, so they must never
# end up in a commit or in a release: the design decisions they inspired are
# recorded as prose in docs/UI_DESIGN.md and docs/TRAININGLAB_UI_PLAN.md, which
# is what actually ships. The folder is permanent locally, invisible to git.
fitness-ecosystem/docs/reference/

# ── Local toolchain ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `.tools/` holds portable binaries that a task needs but that must not live in
# the repository: today the LaTeX engine used to build `docs/ieee/*.pdf`
# (tectonic, one executable, no installation and no admin rights).
.tools/

# ── Local scratch / diagnostics ─────────────────────────────────────────────
.tmp-verify/
# Staging for locally generated exercise images: they are reviewed by hand and
# only then copied into the apps (`generate.mjs --install`). The pose maps that
# feed the model ARE committed — they are our own rig's output and a build input.
fitness-ecosystem/tools/ai-media/out/
*.local
*.log
npm-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# ── Scratch drop folder ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `/ICONS` holds the original artwork the brand masters were cut from. The
# tracked copies live in `fitness-ecosystem/resources/brand/` (that is what the
# icon pipeline reads), so keeping a second copy here only risks them drifting.
/ICONS/

# ── Agent / editor context ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
.freebuff/
.agents/
skills-lock.json
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/settings.json

# ── OS noise ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ── Build output that CI assembles ──────────────────────────────────────────
# `release-artifacts/` is where the release workflow stages installers, and
# `.pages/` is the GitHub Pages bundle (hub + both apps + screenshots).
release-artifacts/
fitness-ecosystem/release-artifacts/
fitness-ecosystem/.pages/

# ── Local databases the LAN server writes ───────────────────────────────────
# The shared store is per-network state (it holds real family data); only the
# schema is source.
*.sqlite
*.sqlite-journal
*.sqlite-wal
*.sqlite-shm
.lan-data/

# ── Secrets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.p12

# ── Personal training data (never commit) ──────────────────────────────────
# Symmetry screenshots used by the OCR importer, plus the cached language data
# tesseract.js downloads on first run.
IMPORT_SYMMETRY/
*.traineddata
fitness-ecosystem/.cache/
